            <ArticleTitle>A conduit between epidemiological research and regional health policy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>250-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1753-6405.12520</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To transform data from a research setting into a format that could be used to support strategies encouraging healthy lifestyle choices and service planning within local government.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Details of the health status and lifestyle behaviours of the Geelong, Victoria, population were generated independently by the Geelong Osteoporosis Study (GOS), a prospective population-based cohort study. Recent GOS follow-up phases provided evidence about patterns of unhealthy diet, physical inactivity, smoking and harmful alcohol use. These factors are well-recognised modifiable risk factors for chronic disease; the dataset was complemented with prevalence estimates for musculoskeletal disease, obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular disease, asthma and cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Data were provided to Healthy Together Geelong in aggregate form according to age, sex and suburb. A population statistics company used the data to project health outcomes by suburb for use by local council. This data exchange served as a conduit between epidemiological research and policy development.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION AND IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Regional policy makers were informed by local evidence, rather than national or state health survey, thereby optimising potential intervention strategies.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 Public Health Association of Australia.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
